This study examines the influence of spin-orbit coupling (SOC) on Young's Ruler (YSR) states arising from magnetic adatoms and dimers. By fitting the angular-dependent YSR spectra of iron adatoms and (100)-oriented iron dimers on the Cu(100) surface, we determined the SOC strength for both monomeric and dimeric configurations. Our results indicate that SOC has a negligible effect on the positions of the YSR peak and splitting observed in the lateral direction on the Cu(100) surface. Conversely, when magnetic adatoms or dimers are rotated out of the Cu(100) plane, the YSR peak position shifts in response to SOC. Specifically, the splitting between the $d_{\uparrow }$ and $d_{\downarrow }$ YSR peaks moves toward lower energies as the SOC increases. Furthermore, the splitting of the YSR peak on the Cu(100) surface is found to be induced by SOC acting upon the anisotropic local magnetic moment. These conclusions are drawn from first-principles calculations based on the density functional theory. Ultimately, the findings presented here offer guidance for interpreting YSR state anisotropies observed on other surface systems.

